pytest's Failed with the mypy error text. Then the gate's red path is observed, not assumed.", "files": ["typecheck/test_mypy.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "The typecheck job's `outcome` output is produced and nothing reads it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. .github/workflows/ci.yml:239 declares `outputs.outcome: ${{ steps.pool.outputs.outcome }}` on the `typecheck` job, but `check` reads only `needs.typecheck.result` (ci.yml:337), and no other job reads `needs.typecheck.outputs`. Nothing goes wrong because of it: the job's own fallback steps read `steps.pool.outputs.outcome` directly. Delete the output, or have `check`'s typecheck step name the route (pool, served, or runner fallback) from it the way the unit verdict step does.", "files": [".github/workflows/ci.yml"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "docs/testing.md says release.yml runs typecheck.sh; release.yml runs raw mypy over 8 of the 32 packages, behind the unit verdict", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The change made typecheck/targets.py the one source of the checked set, but release.yml still carries its own copy, and nothing pins that copy. docs/testing.md:305 (added in this diff) says \"`release.yml` runs `typecheck.sh` on GitHub's runner\". The build plan's last inference says the same. But .github/workflows/release.yml:224-227 runs `uv run mypy --explicit-package-bases -p threetears.core ... -p threetears.datasources` with a hand-written MYPYPATH: 8 roots, not the 32 packages plus example hosts that typecheck/targets.py yields. It is also a step in `check` after \"Every unit shard passed\" with no `!cancelled()`/`always()` guard, so a red shard skips it. That is the exact defect plan Goal 3 fixed in ci.yml. Failure scenario: a maintainer reads docs/testing.md, believes the release gate typechecks the whole family, and changes nothing. A type error in evals, models or iam reaching main through a direct release dispatch goes uncaught by the release gate. Fix with a construction: make release.yml's Typecheck step `./scripts/typecheck.sh` (CI is set, so it runs locally over targets.py's set), gated so a failed unit verdict doesn't skip it. Then add an enforcement assertion that no workflow step under .github/workflows invokes `mypy` directly (search: `mypy` in .github/workflows/*.yml). That makes the doc sentence true and keeps it true.", "files": [".github/workflows/release.yml", "docs/testing.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The pool hand-off contract (TESTAMENT=off reason gate, exit 75 to 2, the no-verdict message) is now copy-pasted into a second script", "recommendation": "Scope: class. scripts/typecheck.sh:71-92 re-implements scripts/test.sh:214-273. Both copy the `command -v testament && -z CI && TESTAMENT != off` dispatch, the TESTAMENT_OFF_REASON refusal (exit 2) and the exit-75-to-2 normalisation, and scripts/test-integration.sh:171-178 has a third copy of the reason gate. scripts/check-all.sh:16-19 and :78-82 read typecheck.sh's and test.sh's exit 2 as the same \"no verdict\", so the scripts must agree, and today they agree only by copy. Failure scenario: a testament release adds another no-verdict exit code, as 0.46 added 75. The maintainer updates test.sh, whose comment at :254-258 is the canonical explanation, and misses typecheck.sh. check-all.sh then stops at the typecheck with that code as if it were a type error, and the runbook's \"Typecheck NOT run\" path never shows. Extract a sourced helper beside scripts/_tool.sh (e.g. a `testament_run <script-name> ...` function that owns the gate, the normalisation and the message) and have test.sh, typecheck.sh and test-integration.sh call it. Search for members: `pool_status -eq 75` and `TESTAMENT=off needs a reason` across scripts/.", "files": ["scripts/typecheck.sh", "scripts/test.sh", "scripts/test-integration.sh", "scripts/check-all.sh"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "CLAUDE.md project content is 196 lines, over the ~150-line budget; this diff lengthens one Scripts row further", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. CLAUDE.md has no PRAWDUCT markers and runs to 196 lines, unchanged in count by this diff. But CLAUDE.md:150 grows the typecheck.sh row into a sentence on the pool and cache semantics, which docs/testing.md \"The typecheck suite, and its cache\" already owns. Consequence: every session loads the extra lines, and the rules that matter (attribution, lockstep versions) get diluted. Trim the row to what to run (`Strict mypy on all packages, or one: ./scripts/typecheck.sh evals`) and link docs/testing.md for the cache. Move the \"Why it keeps happening\" paragraph and the per-surface table of \"No agent attribution\", plus the long test.sh pool paragraphs under Scripts (:155-157), to docs/, leaving the one-line rules and links.", "files": ["CLAUDE.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "ci-unit-pool.sh selects its typecheck behaviour at four scattered sites, one of them sniffing a display string in Python", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Mode is checked at scripts/ci-unit-pool.sh:103 (the message variables), :309 (the testament argv), :389 (dispatch), and in the report heredoc at :354. There the Python re-derives the mode from `sys.argv[2].startswith('unit')`, the human-readable job title. Failure scenario: a future third mode, or a retitled job, gets the wrong summary heading, because nothing ties that heuristic to the bash mode. Put the heading and the testament argv (as an array) in the one per-mode block at :103 and pass the heading to report() verbatim, so mode is decided once. The script's first line (:2, \"CI's unit + enforcement suite... `route` and `unit (testament pool)` jobs\") also no longer names everything the script serves. That is prose, and no edit is needed beyond the restructure.", "files": ["scripts/ci-unit-pool.sh"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The package-to-test-id rule is implemented twice (targets.py test_id and typecheck.sh's substitution), each pinned to a literal, not to each other", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. typecheck/targets.py:46-52 (`Package.test_id`, slashes to dots) names the parametrize ids in typecheck/test_mypy.py. scripts/typecheck.sh:45 rebuilds the same id with `${PACKAGE//\\//.}`, and :37 validates the package with `-d packages/$PACKAGE/src/threetears` rather than membership in `targets.packages()`. tests/test_typecheck_script.py:164 pins the bash side to the literal `[agent.memory]`, and it stubs pytest/testament, so it never checks the id against test_mypy's real parametrization. Failure scenario: test_id changes, or a directory under packages/ has an empty src/threetears tree. `./scripts/typecheck.sh <pkg>` then selects a node id that doesn't exist, pytest exits 4 (\"not found\"), and every test stays green. Remedy: one assertion in tests/enforcement/test_typecheck_targets.py that, for every package in packages(), the selection typecheck.sh would build equals `test_package_typechecks[{p.test_id}]`. Or have typecheck.sh ask targets.py for the id.", "files": ["typecheck/targets.py", "scripts/typecheck.sh", "tests/test_typecheck_script.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The typecheck job's run-here path repeats one route condition on three consecutive steps", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. .github/workflows/ci.yml:272, :276 and :280 carry the identical `!cancelled() && steps.scope.outputs.needed == 'true' && (needs.route.outputs.pool != 'true' || steps.pool.outputs.outcome == 'unreachable')`. tests/enforcement/test_ci_unit_shards.py `_TYPECHECK_HERE` pins all three equal, so they cannot drift today. They are still a new member of the class prior R-5 accepted (the route verdict worked out at several sites), and the pool env block (KEY gate, COORDINATOR, the CONSOLE default URL) is now copied for a third time (:60, :109, :264). A single step (`uv python install 3.14 && uv sync --extra vision && ./scripts/typecheck.sh`) under one `if` removes two copies of the condition and the need for the equality test. The CONSOLE default could move to workflow-level `env:`. No change needed if the per-step split is wanted for log readability.", "files": [".github/workflows/ci.yml", "tests/enforcement/test_ci_unit_shards.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The release gate's typecheck is not typecheck.sh: release.yml:224-227 runs a raw mypy over a hard-coded 8 of the 32 packages, while the plan (line 50) and docs/testing.md:305 state it runs typecheck.sh", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The plan decided to leave release.yml alone because it 'keeps calling typecheck.sh on GitHub's runner (CI set): same tests, run locally'. That premise is false. .github/workflows/release.yml:224-227 (the `check` job's `Typecheck` step) runs `uv run mypy --explicit-package-bases -p threetears.core ... -p threetears.datasources` with a hand-written MYPYPATH: 8 roots, against the suite's 33. It is the same hard-coded list drift that tests/enforcement/test_typecheck_targets.py's docstring gives as the reason the suite reads its targets off the tree, and nothing in that test reads release.yml. The diff then ships the false premise as fact at docs/testing.md:305 ('`release.yml` runs `typecheck.sh` on GitHub's runner'). Failure scenario: a type error in, say, threetears.evals reaches a release commit (a republish via workflow_dispatch, or a main that never ran ci.yml's typecheck on that exact tree). release.yml's `build` waits on a `check` job its own comment describes as waiting for 'lint, typecheck and every unit shard', the step passes, and the release publishes. A maintainer who reads docs/testing.md believes the full set was checked. The owner asked